152935 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153434 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152936 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152937 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152936 #edb/ref 1061 145573 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152936 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c8132653-5a1b-5476-a04e-ce1b65c3f133" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152936 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152086 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152938 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152937 #edb/ref 1061 145576 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f06bd0e-607f-5cfc-b053-5817229eb606" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153447 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152938 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152939 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152938 #edb/ref 1061 145577 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2e9fee2-4bef-5bcf-9273-f2b635d59f5c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152087 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152939 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152940 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152939 #edb/ref 1061 145581 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "daf23f50-7d2e-5502-8fae-1f6557f34364" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153446 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152940 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152941 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152940 #edb/ref 1061 145582 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152940 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8535f2c2-e521-5cfb-8d8c-b5d82a4ebcc6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152940 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152088 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152941 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186152942 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152941 #edb/ref 1061 145585 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152941 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "67c09639-5532-5323-9fdc-8266af5180c4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152941 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153448 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152942 #edb/ref 1061 145586 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152942 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a8d4d64a-55cb-58ec-9d64-db4c619082d1" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186152942 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152089 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1064 "A quantifier is a special symbol (operation) that may transform a predicate into a statement.\n\nThe **universal quantifier** \"$\\forall$\" means \"for all.\" The formula $$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ means that *every* $x$ in the universal set $U$ gives a true statement when substituted into the predicate $P.$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+,\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0$$\n\nreads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **For all** $a \\in \\mathbb{R}^+,$ $\\sqrt{a} > 0$\n\nThis can be further rephrased using plain English as simply:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The square root of any positive real number is positive.*\n\nSometimes, we can omit the \"$\\in U$\" part if the domain is clear from the context and write $\\forall x, \\, P(x).$\n\nFor example, if it's clear that $n$ is an integer, then the following is a valid statement involving the universal quantifier:\n\n$$\\forall n,\\, (2 \\mid n)$$\n\nIn plain English, we might say the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ *The number two divides any integer.*\n\nNote that this statement is *false*." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1065 7991 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6b69eeeb-3757-5954-bd3d-96c7a93010dd" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153434 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153446 #edb/ref 1064 "The existential quantifier \"$\\exists$\" means \"there exists\". The formula\n\n$$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x)$$ means that there is *at least one* element in the universal set $U$ that gives a true statement when substituted into the predicate $P.$\n\nFor example, $$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n$$ reads as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ **There exists** $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ **such that** $2 \\not\\mid n.$\n\nThis can be further rephrased using plain English as simply:\n\n$\\qquad$ *There is an integer that is odd.*\n\nSimilar to the universal quantifier, we can simply write $\\exists x, \\, P(x)$ if $U$ is clear from the context." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153446 #edb/ref 1065 8308 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153446 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a890270-27c1-52c0-a987-6948933acb97" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153446 #edb/ref 1067 "The Existential Quantifier" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153447 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now consider the formal definition of the universal quantifier in connection to predicates of one variable.\n\nThe **universal quantification** of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as $$\\forall x \\in U, \\, P(x).$$ This statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the *entire* universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate equals the universal set $U{:}$ $$T_P = U$$\n\nFor example, consider the quantified statement\n\n$$\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0.$$\n\nNotice that the universal set is $U = \\mathbb{R}$ and the truth set of the underlying predicate\n\n$$P(a): \\:\\: \\sqrt{a} > 0$$\n\nis $T_P = (0,\\infty).$ Since $T_P$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $\\forall a \\in \\mathbb{R},\\, \\sqrt{a} > 0$ is false." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153447 #edb/ref 1065 8309 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153447 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "13634493-c706-51c0-adcf-200f064583eb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153447 #edb/ref 1067 "The Formal Definition of the Universal Quantifier" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1064 "Finally, let's consider the formal definition of the existential quantifier.\n\nThe **existential quantification** of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as $$\\exists x \\in U, \\, P(x).$$ This statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for at least one element $x\\in U,$ i.e., if the truth set $T_P$ of the predicate is not empty: $$T_P \\neq \\emptyset$$\n\nFor example, consider the quantified statement\n\n$$\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n.$$\n\nNotice that the universal set is $U = \\mathbb{Z}$ and the truth set of the underlying predicate\n\n$$P(n): \\:\\: 2 \\not\\mid n$$\n\nis $T_P = \\{\\pm 1,\\pm 3, \\pm 5, \\ldots \\},$ the set of odd numbers. Since $T_P$ is not empty, the statement $\\exists n \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\, 2 \\not\\mid n$ is true." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1065 8310 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aa981bd2-681c-57a1-b6a0-c3b9110e0021" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186153448 #edb/ref 1067 "The Formal Definition of the Existential Quantifier" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407848 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407849 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407850 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407851 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407852 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1045 "Universal and Existential Quantifiers" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186407850 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046679 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050884 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407812 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b645e310-5887-5acb-8fa1-ced6f8dd1a3a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407813 #edb/ref 1081 "There exists $y\\notin Q$ such that $y$ is prime." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407813 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407813 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "03eab922-5a85-5026-a3d7-30ed995fcbfb" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407814 #edb/ref 1081 "Every prime number $y$ is rational." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407814 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407814 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "1e4c46df-0280-55f0-ab7d-60c928de713c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407815 #edb/ref 1081 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407815 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407815 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "2d500fe4-57b9-52b5-83ea-9bda860eab27" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407816 #edb/ref 1081 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407816 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407816 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "327c8c8e-43d3-56fa-bb06-eb3e173dcf46" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407817 #edb/ref 1081 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407817 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407817 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "339444a3-4357-5fdf-8924-442d097e39f0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407818 #edb/ref 1081 "None of the statements are true." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407818 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407818 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "3588a127-8777-5e98-bb9b-b646349772e5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407819 #edb/ref 1081 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407819 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407819 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "424368b9-436c-5519-8a3e-d05dcde6b1f4" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407820 #edb/ref 1081 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407820 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407820 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "45813537-dd63-5755-9b5d-ac39d95f30f1" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407821 #edb/ref 1081 "None of the statements are true." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407821 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 #edb/ref 1759