7592186045585] [17592186407840 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407840 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407840 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407836 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407840 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "7f97a28b-3f8b-5723-9948-a37f37c51db9" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407816 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407821 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407823 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407825 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407833 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407833 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407841 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "8d365a0d-0df0-5d17-b62a-1cd26b68c01b" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407813 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407814 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407824 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407830 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186407832 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186407824 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407842 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "afb31b48-45dd-5d1d-bcf6-b59e8b41fafe" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407841 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183509" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407843 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $P(y):$ $y<y+1⇒{y}^{-1}\\in Q.$\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set ${Z}^{-},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(y)$ is ${Z}^{-}.$\n- $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "25efa028-94e4-52c8-9fb8-df2dc5b08098" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407843 #edb/ref 1074 "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$y<y+1$\" is ${Z}^{-},$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"${y}^{-1}\\in Q$\" is ${Z}^{-}.$\n\nSince $A⇒B≡¬A∨B,$ the truth set of our predicate is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\overset{―}{{Z}^{-}}\\cup {Z}^{-}=∅\\cup {Z}^{-}={Z}^{-}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={Z}^{-}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ coincides with the universal set, the statement $∀y\\in {Z}^{-},\\,P(y)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407839 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407844 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216465" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407844 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407844 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $P(m):$ $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $Z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(m)$ is ${2k:k\\in Z}.$\n- $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "303b12dc-4682-5eea-846a-0e1ea50fc6ae" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407844 #edb/ref 1074 "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer if and only if $m$ is even, we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407842 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183517" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $K(y):$ $y$ is prime\n\nGiven the predicate above, which of the following expresses the statement $∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c6bc667-fec4-55ad-9a19-ce10aa56b016" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407845 #edb/ref 1074 "The existential quantifier \"$∃$\" means \"there exists.\" So, the statement \n$∃y\\in Q,\\,K(y)$ \nreads as follows:\n\n$\\,$ **There exists** $y\\in Q$ **such that** $y$ is prime.\n\n**Note:** This statement is true." #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407846 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407838 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407846 #edb/ref 1069 "q-216495" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407846 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407846 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $P(n):$ $n$ is odd or $n+1$ is odd\n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407846 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5404de7a-e750-5076-ba3f-b3642f799e6f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407846 #edb/ref 1074 "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"$n$ is odd\" is the set of odd natural numbers, and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+1$ is odd\" is the set of even natural numbers.\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={\\text{odd natural numbers}}\\cup {\\text{even natural numbers}}=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407847 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407840 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407847 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183552" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407847 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407847 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ $P(n):$ ${n}^{2}\\in N$ and $n+5>1$ \n\nGiven that the above predicate is defined over the universal set $N,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $∅.$\n- The truth set of $P(n)$ is $N.$\n- $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407847 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ab7419f-dd45-5016-a048-0ef13ef60278" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407847 #edb/ref 1074 "The *existential quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∃x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true for a single element $x\\in U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate is not empty:\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}\\ne ∅\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that:\n\n- the truth set of \"${n}^{2}\\in N$\" is the set $N,$ and\n\n- the truth set of \"$n+5>1$\" is $N.$\n\nSo, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=N\\cap N=N.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}=N.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${T}_{P}$ is *not* empty, the statement $∃n\\in N,\\,P(n)$ is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407851 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1ac83e9e-98ff-5466-a798-9dee1a326560" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407848 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407847 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407849 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407848 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407849 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33b8dae3-4134-5bde-8e80-7adcb9dba955" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407849 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407845 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407850 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407849 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407850 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3a898701-cef3-5e02-9a1d-25cb45a087cd" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407850 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407843 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407851 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186407852 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407851 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4b4b0b11-a323-5bae-b207-166228e36996" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407851 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407844 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407852 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc39dc3f-3c61-5781-b3e2-cef58fc7b9f7" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186407852 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186407846 #edb/ref 17592186045585]}
